<%# Must stay first: a browser only scans the opening bytes of a document for
    the encoding, and anything placed above this competes for that budget. %>
<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
<%
  // Every cover and screenshot on the site is served from the media bucket,
  // which is a different origin — so the first one costs a DNS lookup, a TCP
  // handshake and a TLS negotiation before a single byte of image can move.
  // On a game page views/games/game-detail.ejs preloads the cover and that
  // opens the connection early as a side effect; on every listing page
  // nothing did, and the browser only started the handshake once the parser
  // reached the first <img> — some 200 lines of markup, a navbar and a
  // sidebar later. That wait lands squarely on the largest image in the
  // viewport, which is the element Largest Contentful Paint is measured on.
  //
  // Emitted high in the <head> because the point is to start the handshake
  // before anything else competes for the connection, and below the charset
  // meta for the reason given above it.
  //
  // No `crossorigin`: a plain <img src> is fetched in no-CORS mode, and a
  // browser keeps CORS and non-CORS connections to one origin in separate
  // pools. A preconnect declared crossorigin would open the pool the images
  // never use and leave them to handshake again — the common way to write
  // this tag and have it do nothing.
  //
  // Only where there is artwork to fetch. A connection opened and never used
  // is held idle for ten seconds or so and then dropped, and the legal pages,
  // /about, /how-to-play, /login, /profile and the error views show no game
  // images at all — the sidebars they render are text. `games` covers every
  // listing (the home page, the filters, the curated lists, /most-played) and
  // `game` the detail page and the gallery, which is exactly the set that
  // renders a cover.
  //
  // `locals.mediaOrigin` rather than the bare name the other app.locals are
  // read by, for the reason views/right-sidebar.ejs gives about its own:
  // this partial is rendered directly by the suite with a hand-written set of
  // locals, and a bare reference to one that was not passed is a
  // ReferenceError out of the template rather than an absent tag.
  const hasMedia =
    Boolean(locals.mediaOrigin) &&
    Boolean(
      locals.game || (Array.isArray(locals.games) && locals.games.length > 0),
    );
%>
<% if (hasMedia) { %>
<link rel="preconnect" href="<%= locals.mediaOrigin %>" />
<% } %>
<meta name="csrf-token" content="<%= locals.csrfToken %>" />
<%#
     Google Analytics, on every visit. High in <head> so a hit is recorded
     before a visitor can navigate away again, but below the charset meta,
     which has to stay first.

     An EJS comment rather than an HTML one, which is what this used to be:
     <!-- --> is copied into the response, so every word here was shipped to
     every visitor and every crawler on every page view. None of it is for
     them — and the consent paragraphs below least of all, which have no
     business being published as part of the page they describe.

     The ad signals are denied explicitly: this site runs no advertising and
     has no use for them. analytics_storage is left alone, so it keeps gtag's
     own default of granted and hits are reported normally — a "denied" there
     would switch the tag to cookieless pings, which feed modelling rather
     than the plain visitor count this is here for. The call has to precede
     gtag("js"), or it arrives too late to apply.

     Worth knowing if the numbers ever go quiet: these defaults can also be
     set on the tag itself, in GA under Admin -> Data streams -> Configure tag
     settings, and denying analytics_storage there would zero out collection
     for every European visitor without a line of this file changing.

     There is no consent banner, and that is a decision rather than an
     oversight — written down here because the alternative is that every
     review rediscovers it and proposes the same fix.

     Analytics cookies are not strictly necessary, so the ePrivacy Directive
     wants them asked about *before* they are set; the Czech implementation
     (Act No. 127/2005 Sb.) has been opt-in since 2022. Nothing here asks.
     Both ways of closing that were weighed and both were declined:

       - a banner, which the operator does not want on the site;
       - denying analytics_storage above, which removes the cookie outright
         and so removes the question with it, at the cost of the plain
         visitor count this tag exists for (see the paragraph above).

     What is left is a disclosed, accepted risk, and the disclosure is real:
     views/privacy-policy.ejs names these cookies, says plainly that they are
     set without asking, and tells the reader how to refuse them. README.md
     records the decision under "A note on analytics and cookie consent".

     Reopen it if the trade changes — if the site starts advertising, if it
     begins collecting anything beyond page views, or if modelled numbers
     become good enough after all. Until then this is known, not missed.
%>
<script async nonce="<%= locals.cspNonce %>" src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-76SK68FN8Y"></script>
<script nonce="<%= locals.cspNonce %>">
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];

  function gtag() {
    dataLayer.push(arguments);
  }

  gtag("consent", "default", {
    ad_storage: "denied",
    ad_user_data: "denied",
    ad_personalization: "denied",
  });

  gtag("js", new Date());

  // No "anonymize_ip": that is a Universal Analytics parameter and GA4
  // ignores it. GA4 truncates the address on collection unconditionally.
  gtag("config", "G-76SK68FN8Y");
</script>
<%
  // One tag, not two. A URL carrying both a search and a sort — which the
  // filter controls produce together — used to emit "noindex, follow" twice.
  //
  // locals.noindex is the other half: pages that are never worth indexing
  // whatever their query string — the login form, the legal boilerplate, a
  // gallery slide, page 2 of the comment archive. Each of those used to write
  // a robots tag of its own further down its own <head>, which put the
  // decision out of reach of the canonical tag below. The gallery is what
  // that cost: it canonicalises to the game page, so it emitted a noindex and
  // a canonical pointing elsewhere together — the one pairing the comment on
  // that tag calls conflicting. Routed through here the two cannot disagree,
  // because one variable decides both.
  // The expression this used to spell out lives in utils/indexability.ts now.
  // It was never only this file's decision: every listing view writes a JSON-LD
  // node of its own and emitted it whatever the robots tag beside it said, so
  // the same question was being answered in two places with two copies of the
  // rule. One function, one answer, and a view can ask it too.
  const noindex = isNoindex(req, locals);
%>
<% if (noindex) { %>
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow" />
<% } else { %>
<%#
     The other half of the same decision, and it used to be nothing at all: a
     page that wanted indexing emitted no robots tag, which reads as "no
     preferences stated" — and the defaults a crawler falls back to are not the
     ones this site wants.

     An EJS comment rather than an HTML one, for the reason the analytics block
     above gives about its own: <!-- --> is copied into the response, so a
     paragraph of reasoning written for whoever edits this file next would
     otherwise be shipped to every visitor and every crawler on every page
     view. This branch runs on all of them.

     max-image-preview:large is the one that costs something to leave unsaid.
     Article 15 of the EU Copyright Directive makes showing a preview of
     someone else's content a licensable act, and Google's answer is to serve
     EU users a thumbnail unless the site opts in with this exact directive.
     That is the whole of what this catalogue is: cover art and screenshots,
     submitted to image search through the image:loc entries routes/sitemap.ts
     writes, on a .eu domain whose audience is precisely the one the
     restriction applies to. So the site was going to the trouble of publishing
     an image sitemap and then having those images shown at thumbnail size to
     the people most likely to be looking for them.

     max-snippet:-1 lifts the cap on the text snippet for the same reason —
     stated, rather than left to a default that varies by jurisdiction. The
     descriptions here are written to be read in a result (see
     META_DESCRIPTION_MAX and the routes that compose them against it), which
     is an odd amount of care to take over a sentence and then let it be cut by
     a policy nobody on this side chose.

     There is no max-video-preview. The directive is real and Google reads it,
     but no page on this site carries video, so it would be a third value
     shipped on every request for a crawler to parse and discard — the same
     objection routes/sitemap.ts makes to changefreq and priority, and
     routes/home.ts to the keywords tag.

     Deliberately not "index, follow" alongside these. Both are already the
     default, and stating a default is how the tags in this file came to
     contradict each other in the first place: `noindex` above is the one place
     that decides this, and a tag asserting "index" in the branch where that is
     false adds no information while giving a future edit something to
     disagree with.
%>
<meta name="robots" content="max-image-preview:large, max-snippet:-1" />
<% } %>
<%# The file is a PNG and was declared as an .ico, which is a different format
    entirely. Browsers sniff it and cope, but there is no reason to make them. %>
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon.png" />
<%#
     The icon iOS uses when a visitor adds the site to their home screen, and
     the one Safari shows beside a bookmark.

     Without it iOS takes a screenshot of the page instead, which on a site
     this dark is a thumbnail of a blue rectangle. There is no separate file
     for it: /images/logo.png is the 360x360 mark utils/site.ts already keeps
     for the Organization node, and it is the right shape for this too —
     Apple wants 180x180, scales anything larger down, and requires an opaque
     image, which that one is (SITE_LOGO explains why it was flattened onto
     the site's background rather than left transparent).

     No `sizes` attribute. It is only worth writing when several files are
     offered and iOS has to choose between them; with one, it is a second
     thing that can disagree with the pixels behind it.
%>
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/images/logo.png" />
<%#
     The browser chrome's colour, and the manifest that names it again for an
     installed window.

     Neither is a ranking signal and neither is here pretending to be one. The
     site is themed top to bottom after a DOS file manager and then handed the
     phone a white address bar above it, which is the one part of the screen
     the design never reached.

     The value is the classic palette's --nc-bg (see :root in style.css), which
     is what .navbar's top row paints itself — so the chrome continues the bar
     directly beneath it rather than approximating it.

     It cannot be rendered correctly from here, and that is worth understanding
     before someone tries: the chosen theme lives in localStorage and only the
     browser knows it, so this tag ships as the classic blue on every response
     and public/js/theme-switcher.js corrects it for the other two. That script
     already runs synchronously in <head> for exactly this class of problem —
     it puts the theme on <html> before anything is painted — so the correction
     lands with the same timing. The meta is emitted *above* it deliberately:
     the script reaches for this element by name, and the parser has to have
     seen it first.

     The manifest is a static file rather than a route, which is the opposite
     of what routes/sitemap.ts decided for robots.txt — the difference being
     that robots.txt has to name the canonical host in its Sitemap line, and
     every address in a manifest resolves relative to the manifest itself.
     There is nothing in it that depends on where the site is deployed. It is
     "minimal-ui" rather than "standalone" because this is a catalogue people
     browse — an installed window with no back button is the wrong shape for
     it. tests/public-assets.test.ts keeps its name and its icons honest
     against utils/site.ts and against the files on disk, the same way that
     suite already keeps public/js-dos.html honest about the media origin.
%>
<meta name="theme-color" content="#0000aa" />
<link rel="manifest" href="/site.webmanifest" />
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/vt323.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/orbitron.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= asset('/css/style.css') %>" />
<%# Only on a page that asks to be indexed. Everything the block above marks
    noindex used to name a *different* address here — "/" for a search, the
    unsorted page for a sort, the game page for a gallery slide — which is
    the one combination Google explicitly calls conflicting: a canonical
    pointing away says "index that one instead", the noindex says "index
    nothing", and which of the two wins is then anybody's guess. Saying
    nothing here leaves the noindex to speak for itself. %>
<% if (!noindex) { %>
<link rel="canonical" href="<%= locals.canonicalUrl || (siteUrl + req.originalUrl.split('?')[0]) %>" />
<% } %>
<%
  // Built here rather than by each route, so a page cannot have a visible
  // trail and no schema for it — which is what every listing page had. Gated
  // on the same `noindex` as the canonical above: a page that asks not to be
  // indexed has no use for a rich result, and the gallery (noindex, and
  // rendered with a `game` local) would otherwise claim a breadcrumb for a
  // page that is not the game page.
  const crumbLdJson = noindex
    ? null
    : breadcrumbLdJson(locals.breadcrumbs || buildBreadcrumbs(locals));
%>
<% if (crumbLdJson) { %>
<script type="application/ld+json"><%- JSON.stringify(crumbLdJson).replace(/</g, "\\u003c") %></script>
<% } %>
<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="OldSchoolGames — New games" href="<%= siteUrl %>/feed.xml" />
<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="OldSchoolGames — News" href="<%= siteUrl %>/news/feed.xml" />
<%#
     The paging hints, and what they are and are not worth — written down for
     the same reason the SearchAction in views/index.ejs is, which is that the
     obvious next move is the wrong one.

     Google announced in March 2019 that it had not used rel=prev/next for
     indexing in years and dropped it from its documentation, so nothing here
     should be read as a Google signal. Deleting the pair anyway would be the
     mistake: Bing has said it still reads them, the relations belong to the
     HTML spec rather than to anybody's search product, and a browser or a
     reader mode can chain and prefetch a sequence from them. Two link elements
     on a paginated page is a cheap price for that.

     What actually carries a paginated set to Google is the rest of what
     utils/pagination.ts arranges: a self-referencing canonical on every page,
     a numbered title element and description so the deeper pages are not
     near-copies of the first, and a nextPageUrl derived from the row count so
     the chain never points at a page that answers 404. Those were always doing
     the work; these two are the standards-compliant extra.

     Only on a page that asks to be indexed, the same condition the canonical
     is written under. The routes build prevPageUrl and nextPageUrl from the
     unfiltered listing — that is what the canonical names — so on a search or
     a re-sort they described a different sequence from the one on screen:
     "/?search=doom&page=2" said its previous page was the homepage and its
     next "/?page=3", pages of a list that was not being paginated. A noindex
     page has no sequence worth announcing to the consumers that read these.
%>
<% if (!noindex && locals.prevPageUrl) { %>
<link rel="prev" href="<%= locals.prevPageUrl %>" />
<% } %>
<% if (!noindex && locals.nextPageUrl) { %>
<link rel="next" href="<%= locals.nextPageUrl %>" />
<% } %>
<%# theme-switcher.js is the one script here that must block.

    It reads the stored theme and puts the class on <html> before anything
    is painted; deferred, the page would render in the default palette and
    then repaint into the chosen one on every single navigation. It is the
    smallest of the three for exactly that reason.

    The other two do not need the parser to wait: favorites.js registers
    nothing until it is called, and ui.js attaches its delegated listener to
    the document, which a deferred script still does before DOMContentLoaded
    fires. Loaded bare they were some 30 KB of render-blocking script in the
    <head> of every page on the site. `defer` preserves execution order
    between them, so the only thing that changes is when it happens. %>
<script src="<%= asset('/js/theme-switcher.js') %>"></script>
<script defer src="<%= asset('/js/favorites.js') %>"></script>
<script defer src="<%= asset('/js/ui.js') %>"></script>
<%# Here rather than beside the other og: tags in each layout, because unlike
    them it is the same on every page: the site is written in one language and
    has no translations. Without it a scraper has to guess the locale of a
    shared link from the text. %>
<meta property="og:locale" content="en_US" />
<%# Twitter card %>
<%#
     These read `locals`, which is a contract with the views that include this
     partial, and it is worth stating because eight of them used to break it.

     Most pages on the site are handed a title and a description by their
     route, so `locals.title` is set before this file runs and the card agrees
     with the title element below it. The error views, /profile, /login and the
     two game forms did it the other way round: each declared the pair as a
     template const underneath the line that pulls this partial in. A const in
     the including template is a variable in that template's compiled function
     and never a property of the data object handed down here, and it was
     declared after the partial had already rendered besides. So this tag fell
     through to the fallback, and every one of those pages advertised the
     site's name where its own title sat two lines further down.

     Nothing was actually lost: all eight are noindex, and the og: tags beside
     them are written after the const and were always correct. It is fixed
     because it was a trap rather than a bug — the day one of those pages
     becomes indexable, the card silently disagrees with the page.

     Those views declare the pair above that line now and pass it down as the
     partial's second argument, which EJS merges over the parent's data.
     tests/views/meta-tags.test.ts holds both halves for every view, so the
     ordering cannot quietly come back.

     Note for whoever edits this paragraph: that suite reads these files as
     text, so spelling the title element or the partial call literally here
     would register this comment as a page of its own.
%>
<%#
     The card type, which was the string "summary_large_image" on every page of
     the site and was wrong on exactly the pages that get shared.

     X renders a large card only if the image is at least 300x157; below that
     it falls back to the small card, or to no image at all. The comment on
     twitter:image below already tells this story about the old 180x180
     navbar.webp fallback — but it ends "except on a game page, where a cover
     is used instead", and that exception was never checked. The covers in the
     media bucket are 150 pixels wide: 150x195 for Might and Magic, 150x205 for
     Space Quest I, 150x207 for Ultima IV. Every one of them is under half the
     width X needs. So the fallback was fixed and the case it was excused for
     is the case that still fails — on the game pages, which are the ones
     anybody actually links to.

     Raising the declaration to meet the image is not an option here: 150 pixels
     is what the art is. A DOS-era box scan at 1200 wide would be an upscale,
     and the site has no image pipeline to make one with. So the declaration
     comes down to meet the art instead. `summary` is a real card — title,
     description and a square thumbnail of the cover — and it is what X was
     already falling back to on these pages, minus the chance of it deciding to
     drop the picture entirely.

     Keyed on `locals.image` because that is the same switch views/og-image.ejs
     already turns: a page either supplies its own artwork, which on this site
     is always a game cover from the bucket, or it takes `siteImage`, which is
     the 1200x630 file the large card was written for. The two tags cannot
     disagree about which of those is in play.

     Worth reopening if the covers ever grow. If the bucket starts holding art
     at 600 wide or more, this reverts to one string again.
     tests/views/twitter-card.test.ts asserts the split and carries the
     measurements, so that is the file to read before changing it back — the
     numbers live in the media bucket rather than in the repository, which is
     why nothing here can check them for itself.

     Open Graph needs no equivalent. Facebook and LinkedIn have one card and
     size the image into it; there is no declaration there that can contradict
     the file.
%>
<meta name="twitter:card" content="<%= locals.image ? "summary" : "summary_large_image" %>" />
<meta name="twitter:title" content="<%= locals.title || siteTitle %>" />
<meta name="twitter:description" content="<%= locals.description || siteDescription %>" />
<%# The fallback is `siteImage` — a 1200x630 file — and used to be
    /images/navbar.webp, which is 180x180. The card above declares
    `summary_large_image` on every page of the site, and X wants 300x157 at
    the very least before it will render one: below that it quietly downgrades
    to the small card, or shows no image at all. So the one declaration and
    the one image contradicted each other everywhere except on a game page,
    where a cover is used instead. See SITE_IMAGE in utils/site.ts. %>
<%# Absolute for the reason views/og-image.ejs gives: a cover stored as a path
    on this site is fine for an <img> and invisible to the X card scraper. %>
<meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= locals.image ? absoluteUrl(locals.image) : siteImage %>" />
<%# The card had an image and nothing describing it, which is what a screen
    reader on Twitter/X reads out as an unlabelled attachment. The page title
    is what the picture is of — views/og-image.ejs writes the Open Graph half
    of this pair from the same expression. It used to be written in
    game-detail.ejs alone, which left every other page's card with a picture
    and no label.

    The same expression is the same value only while both partials are
    handed the same `title`. A view that declares its own (the error pages,
    /profile, /login) passes it to og-image.ejs as well as to this file;
    six of them passed it here alone, and their Open Graph alt fell back to
    the site's name. tests/views/share-image-alt.test.ts renders them.

    There is no twitter:image:width/height to go with it because X defines no
    such properties; the Open Graph pair that does exist is emitted by
    views/og-image.ejs, and only for the image whose size is known. %>
<meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="<%= locals.title || siteTitle %>" />
<meta name="twitter:site" content="@RobertLibsansky" />
<meta name="twitter:creator" content="@RobertLibsansky" />
